## The most common modelling mistake

Optional fields are used to represent a state machine:

```ts
interface SpecimenResult {          // this allows nonsense
  status: string;
  value?: number;
  error?: string;
  isLoading?: boolean;              // loading AND an error AND a value at the same time?
}
```

This type says which fields may be absent **individually**. It cannot say which combinations are legal. So it permits a result that is loading, failed, and complete simultaneously, and it permits a `'complete'` result with no value at all.

Every consumer of this type inherits the job of defending against combinations that should not exist.

## The alternative

```ts
type SpecimenResult =               // only real states can exist
  | { status: 'pending' }
  | { status: 'running'; startedAt: string }
  | { status: 'complete'; value: number; units: string }
  | { status: 'failed'; error: string; retryable: boolean };
```

A **discriminated union** is a set of variants separated by one shared field holding a literal value. Here that field is `status`, and it is called the **discriminant** or **tag**.

Once you check the tag, TypeScript gives you exactly the fields that variant has. Reading `result.value` on a `'failed'` result is now a compile error rather than a runtime crash.

Notice what disappeared: `isLoading` is gone, because loading is a *state* (`'pending'` or `'running'`) rather than a flag that coexists with other states. That collapse is typical. Boolean flags alongside a status field are almost always states in disguise.

## Exhaustiveness checking

The union alone is valuable. Combined with an exhaustiveness check it becomes something stronger — a mechanism that tells you, at build time, every place a domain change affects.

```ts
function label(r: SpecimenResult): string {
  switch (r.status) {
    case 'pending':  return 'Queued';
    case 'running':  return `Started ${r.startedAt}`;
    case 'complete': return `${r.value} ${r.units}`;
    case 'failed':   return r.error;
    default: {
      const _exhaustive: never = r;      // a new state causes a compile error here
      return _exhaustive;
    }
  }
}
```

How it works: inside the `default` branch, TypeScript has narrowed `r` by eliminating every case you handled. If you handled all of them, what remains is `never`, and assigning `never` to `never` is fine. Add a fifth variant and the remaining type is no longer `never`, so the assignment fails — in every switch that needs updating, at once.

This is the highest-value pattern in the section. It reappears in Section 7 as the four async states, and in Section 8 as the form submission states.

workedExamples.problem:
A pathology console models a specimen result with optional fields. Convert it to a discriminated union with an exhaustiveness check, and count what the conversion removed.

```ts
interface SpecimenResult {
  status: string;
  value?: number;
  units?: string;
  error?: string;
  retryable?: boolean;
  startedAt?: string;
  isLoading?: boolean;
}

function renderResult(r: SpecimenResult) {
  if (r.status !== 'failed') {
    return `${r.value!.toFixed(2)} ${r.units}`;
  }
  return r.error;
}
```

steps:
Write the union, giving each variant a literal tag:

```ts
type SpecimenResult =
  | { status: 'pending' }
  | { status: 'running'; startedAt: string }
  | { status: 'complete'; value: number; units: string }
  | { status: 'failed'; error: string; retryable: boolean };
```

Rewrite the consumer as a switch on the tag, with no assertions:

```ts
function renderResult(r: SpecimenResult): string {
  switch (r.status) {
    case 'pending':  return 'Queued';
    case 'running':  return `Started ${r.startedAt}`;
    case 'complete': return `${r.value.toFixed(2)} ${r.units}`;
    case 'failed':   return r.error;
    default: {
      const _exhaustive: never = r;
      return _exhaustive;
    }
  }
}
```

