## External data is `unknown`

Data that enters your application from the network, from `localStorage`, or from a URL is **`unknown`**, whatever the type annotation claims.

Writing `const user = await res.json() as User` is a claim the compiler will accept without checking. It performs no runtime work, and TypeScript erases entirely at build time, so nothing inspects the actual value.

The failure then surfaces three components later as `Cannot read properties of undefined`, far from the line that caused it.

## Validate once, at the edge

```ts
const Specimen = z.object({
  id: z.string(),
  collectedAt: z.string().datetime(),
  result: z.discriminatedUnion('status', [
    z.object({ status: z.literal('pending') }),
    z.object({ status: z.literal('running'), startedAt: z.string() }),
    z.object({ status: z.literal('complete'), value: z.number(), units: z.string() }),
    z.object({ status: z.literal('failed'), error: z.string(), retryable: z.boolean() }),
  ]),
});
type Specimen = z.infer<typeof Specimen>;    // one source of truth

export async function getSpecimen(id: string): Promise<Specimen> {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/specimens/${id}`);
  if (!res.ok) throw new ApiError(res.status);
  return Specimen.parse(await res.json());    // it fails here, not three screens later
}
```

Note the `res.ok` check from Section 1 sitting immediately above the shape check. Both are the same boundary asking two questions: *did the request succeed*, and *is the answer the shape I expect*. Neither is optional, and code that has one without the other is usually a defect waiting for a bad day.

