## The argument-swap defect

`patientId` and `specimenId` are both `string`. So passing one where the other belongs compiles cleanly and fails in production — usually as a query that returns nothing, or worse, a query that returns the wrong record.

A **brand** makes them different types at compile time. It costs nothing at runtime, because the brand exists only in the type system.

```ts
type Brand<T, B> = T & { readonly __brand: B };
type PatientId  = Brand<string, 'PatientId'>;
type SpecimenId = Brand<string, 'SpecimenId'>;

declare function getResults(id: SpecimenId): Promise<Specimen[]>;
getResults(patientId);        // ✗ compile error — previously a silent wrong query
```

## Where branded values are created

Create them at the **validation boundary**, where external data is checked anyway:

```ts
const Specimen = z.object({
  id: z.string().transform(s => s as SpecimenId),
  patientId: z.string().transform(s => s as PatientId),
});
```

The alternative — casting wherever a branded value is needed — scatters `as SpecimenId` through the codebase and defeats the purpose, because a cast is exactly the thing that lets the wrong value through.

Use this wherever two identifiers of the same primitive type pass through the same functions. That describes almost every business domain: order and customer, account and transaction, patient and specimen.

