## Four triggers, and only four

A component re-renders when:

1. its own state changed;
2. a context value it consumes changed;
3. **its parent re-rendered**;
4. a hook it uses signalled a change.

The third one surprises people. **When a parent re-renders, its children re-render by default**, whether or not their props changed. React does not compare props unless you explicitly opt in with `memo`.

This is why a state change high in the tree can cause a large number of renders. It is also why the correct first response is usually not `memo` but moving the state closer to where it is used — which is Section 6's placement rule, and the reason blast radius is one of the five questions there.

workedExamples.problem:
A check-in console has a search field at the top of the page. Typing one character causes the profiler to show 340 components rendering. Work out why, and decide what to do — without yet reaching for memoization.

```tsx
