## Custom hooks are the unit of logic reuse

Not higher-order components. Not render props. A hook returns state and handlers, and any markup can consume it:

```tsx
function useSeatSelection(flightId: FlightId) {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState<SeatId | null>(null);
  const toggle = useCallback((id: SeatId) =>
    setSelected(cur => (cur === id ? null : id)), []);
  return { selected, toggle };
}
```

Note the functional update inside `toggle` — it is what allows the empty dependency array, because the updater does not need to capture `selected`.

## Headless components go further

A **headless** component owns behaviour and state and renders no markup at all. The caller provides everything visual.

This is how TanStack Table, React Hook Form, and Radix primitives work, and it is why they survive redesigns: a visual overhaul changes the caller's markup and touches none of the behaviour. Section 9 returns to this when tables need both complex behaviour and complete control over accessibility markup.

