## The wrong question

Section 3 showed a `variant` prop chosen from viewport width, and showed it breaking in a 300 px sidebar on a 1440 px screen — because a media query asks about the **viewport**, and the component's real constraint is **the space it was given**. Those are not the same question, and a component placed in a sidebar or a modal is answering the wrong one.

A container query asks the right question directly.

```css
.exhibition-list { container-type: inline-size; container-name: exhibition-list; }

.exhibition-card { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-3); }

@container exhibition-list (min-width: 34rem) {
  .exhibition-card { grid-template-columns: 12rem 1fr; }
  .exhibition-card__summary { display: block; }
}
```

The card now describes itself. It adapts wherever it is placed — a wide main column, a narrow sidebar, a modal — with **no prop, no `ResizeObserver`, and no state.** The consumer's only job is to establish a container with `container-type`.

