## Cascade layers end the specificity arms race

A utility class fails to override a component rule, so a developer writes `!important`. That wins the current conflict and raises the ceiling permanently — the next override now needs its own `!important`, and the one after that has nothing left to escalate to.

**`@layer` declares precedence directly, and layer order beats specificity outright.**

```css
@layer reset, tokens, base, components, utilities;
/* Any rule in `utilities` wins over any rule in `components`,
   regardless of how specific the component selector is. */
```

A single-class utility declared inside the `utilities` layer overrides a deeply nested component selector declared inside `components`, with no `!important` anywhere. This is the same idea Section 1 applied to code — a layered structure with an enforced direction — expressed in CSS's own cascade mechanism.

