## Mock at the network boundary

Mocking your own API module replaces your code with your own assumption about what it does:

```ts
jest.mock('./api/refunds', () => ({ getBalance: () => Promise.resolve({ balance: 4000 }) }));
```

If the real endpoint returns `balancePence` and your mock returns `balance`, this test passes and production fails — the test never touched the real fetch layer, cache keys, serialisation, or error handling at all.

Intercepting at HTTP leaves all of that genuinely under test:

```ts
const server = setupServer(
  http.get('/api/cards/:id', ({ params }) =>
    HttpResponse.json({ id: params.id, balancePence: 4000 })),
);
beforeAll(() => server.listen({ onUnhandledRequest: 'error' }));
afterEach(() => server.resetHandlers());
```

